<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samoan Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Samoa Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Samoan Islands are an archipelago of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> in the central South Pacific, settled about 3,000 years ago. Rival chiefly titles competed for power there, with Tongan influence in some periods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripartite-convention-1899">Tripartite Convention of 1899</a> divided the islands: the western part, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a> and Savaii, became German Samoa, later administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and independent as Samoa in 1962, while the eastern part, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutuila">Tutuila</a> and Manuʻa, became American Samoa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samoan-islands.jpg" alt="Samoan Islands" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Angela K. Kepler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olosega_Aerial_AKK.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
